uted, or the call is in a different bank? SMB is 32KB PRG, only one bank. Search covered 32768 bytes.\n\nHmm, let me reconsider the PC convention. Maybe trace reports the PC of the instruction itself (not next). Then the STA $2007 would be at $8EBE, not $8EBB. Then the loop: $8EBC dex / $8EBD bne. Let's test both alignments.\n\nAt $8EBB bytes: from disassembly listing, $8EBB: 8d 07 20 (sta $2007), $8EBE: ca (dex), $8EBF: d0 f5 (bne). So STA $2007 at $8EBB, dex at $8EBE. Trace PC = $8EBE. If trace reported instruction PC, the write would be from $8EBE = dex, impossible. So trace reports post-instruction PC (the address of the next instruction after the store completes). Actually in 6502, the program counter during the store cycle points at the next instruction. So PC $8EBE means the store instruction began at $8EBB. Good, matches.\n\nNow for $2006 trace PCs: $8E95 after STA $2006 at $8E92. Good.\n\nSo the routine entry $8E92. But no callers. That's weird but possible if the routine is dispatched via a jump table of routine addresses. But search \"92 8E\" would find a 16-bit pointer entry in a table. None. So the pointer is computed at runtime, OR the routine is actually reached by falling through from code before $8E92 that I mis-attributed.\n\nLet me read the exact bytes around $8E80-$8E95 to verify there isn't a different instruction boundary. Perhaps $8E8C isn't actually `rts` and the real flow falls into $8E92. Let me read prgRom 0xE80.\n\nActually, the more likely resolution: The routine is called indirectly. In SMB, the NMI's VRAM buffer write is invoked via `JSR $8E92`? Hmm.\n\nWait, actually I recall now: In SMB the NMI handler ends with `JMP $8057`? No. Let me recall the structure. Actually the main NMI is at $8082 and there's a part `JSR WriteVRAMBuffer`. Hmm.\n\nLet me just disassemble the entire NMI from $8082 to see all JSRs.\n\nActually, maybe the relevant detail: the search pattern \"20 92 8E\" — maybe the call uses JSR to $8E92 but the bytes are diffe