EDD: LDX $2002      ; reset PPU latch\n$8EE0: LDY #$00\n$8EE2: LDA ($00),Y    ; first byte of buffer\n$8EE4: BNE $8E92      ; if nonzero -> do transfer\n$8EE6: STA $2005      ; else (zero = end): reset scroll\n$8EE9: STA $2005\n$8EEC: RTS\n```\nSo if the buffer's first byte is 0 (empty), it just resets scroll and returns. Otherwise branch to $8E92:\n```\n$8E92: STA $2006      ; store A (first byte = PPU addr high) to $2006\n$8E95: INY\n$8E96: LDA ($00),Y    ; second byte = PPU addr low\n$8E98: STA $2006\n$8E9B: INY\n$8E9C: LDA ($00),Y    ; third byte = control/length: bit7 = vertical increment, bits 0-5 = count\n$8E9E: ASL A          ; shift bit7 into carry\n$8E9F: PHA\n$8EA0: LDA $0778      ; PPUCTRL shadow\n$8EA3: ORA #$04\n$8EA5: BCS $8EA9      ; if vertical flag set, keep ORA #$04\n$8EA7: AND #$FB       ; else clear bit2 (horizontal increment)\n$8EA9: JSR $8EED      ; EnableNMI: STA $2000 / STA $0778 / RTS  (updates PPUCTRL)\n$8EAC: PLA\n$8EAD: ASL A          ; shift again\n$8EAE: BCC $8EB2      ; if bit6 (repeat) not set ...\n$8EB0: ORA #$02       ; set bit1 = horizontal repeat/VRAM increment 1?\n$8EB2: INY\n$8EB3: LSR A\n$8EB4: LSR A\n$8EB5: TAX            ; X = count (bits 0-5)\n$8EB6: BCS $8EB8      ; branch if...\n$8EB8: INY\n$8EB9: LDA ($00),Y    ; load data byte\n$8EBB: STA $2007      ; write to PPU\n$8EBE: DEX\n$8EBF: BNE $8EB8      ; loop count times\n$8EC1: SEC\n$8EC2: TYA\n$8EC3: ADC $00\n$8EC5: STA $00        ; advance buffer pointer by Y\n$8EC7: LDA #$00\n$8EC9: ADC $01\n$8ECB: STA $01\n$8ECD: LDA #$3F\n$8ECF: STA $2006      ; set PPU addr $3F00 (palette)\n$8ED2: LDA #$00\n$8ED4: STA $2006\n$8ED7: STA $2006\n$8EDA: STA $2006      ; wait, three stores of 0? \n$8EDD: LDX $2002\n$8EE0: LDY #$00\n$8EE2: LDA ($00),Y\n$8EE4: BNE $8E92      ; loop back for next record\n...\n```\nWait but $8ED4/$8ED7/$8EDA STA $2006 three times writes 0 to $2006 three times (PPU addr $0000 then...) Hmm, that seems odd. Actually maybe it's STA $2006 then something. Let me look again: \n$8ECD: a9 3f LDA #$3F\n$8ECF: 8d 06 20 STA $2006\n$8ED2: a9 00 LDA #$00\n$8ED4: 8d 06 20 STA $2006\n$8ED7: 8d 06 20 STA $2006\n$8EDA: 8d 06 20 STA $2006\nHmm three STAs of 0. Actually wait, that can't be right. Let me re-read: the disassembly output shows:\n```\n$8ecd: a9 3f       lda #0x3f\n$8ecf: 8d 06 20    sta 0x2006\n$8ed2: a9 00       lda #0x00\n$8ed4: 8d 06 20    sta 0x2006\n$8ed7: 8d 06 20    sta 0x2006\n$8eda: 8d 06 20    sta 0x2006\n$8edd: ae 02 20    ldx 0x2002\n```\nHmm. Maybe the last few are actually $2005? Let me check the raw bytes. Actually $8ED7 could be `8d 05 20` (STA $2005) and $8EDA `8d 05 20`. The disassembler showed 06 for all three, but I should verify raw bytes. Let me read prgRom 0xECD length 0x20.\n\nActually this matters for accuracy of the note but not hugely. Let me read raw bytes to be precise.\n\nAlso, the two trace $2006 writers at $8ED7 and $8EDA (PCs) came from the trace, and values 0. So they ARE writes to $2006 ($2006 address 8198). The trace said writers at PC $8ED7 and $8EDA with address 8198 = 0x2006. Yes so all three are $2006 writes. So it writes $3F then $00 then $00 then $00 → sets PPU address to $3F00 and then to $0000+$0000 = $0000? Actually writing $2006 twice sets the address: first $3F, then $00 → $3F00. The additional two writes of $00 would set the address to $0000 (since latch resets). Hmm, odd but that's what it does — it resets the PPU address to $0000 after the transfer, likely to restore the scroll/address for the PPU. Actually a common SMB idiom: after transferring, it sets PPU address to $3F00 then writes twice more to reset the address latch to $0000 so the PPU's VRAM address ends at $0000 (so the nametable/scroll is fine). Actually setting to $0000 makes the PPU render nametable 0 at top-left. This is done because during transfer the PPU address register was changed; resetting to $0000 restores it.\n\nOK. So the \"routine\" = WriteVRAMBuffer, entry $8EDD (the JSR target from NMI). Hmm but the question: \"find the routine that copies queued writes into the PPU during the NMI (the code that writes $2006 and $2007 from a buffer). Prove it with trace_writes on $2007 ... to get the writing PC, then disassemble back to the routine's entry.\"\n\nSo which entry do we record? The canonical SMB label \"WriteVRAMBuffer\" — I should determine. Let me think about the actual disa","truncated":true}
